THIS ENTRY IS THE FOURTH INSTANCE OF ONE MISTAKE, and it is the one that matters.
The section below used to close this file with a blanket — "everything else in
the tree" — which swept in the GT artwork's siblings, then the OFL fonts, and
then this. For one commit, the file whose entire purpose is to stop this project
granting what it does not hold offered a stranger the right to SELL A CHILD'S
PAINTINGS. A blanket affirmative in a carve-out is not a tidy ending; it is an
unbounded grant over everything the author did not happen to think of, and the
author will always fail to think of something. Withholdings get named. Never
blanketed.


WHAT IS NOT COVERED BY ANY ENTRY ABOVE

Everything not named above is this project's own work and is offered under
LICENSE — named that way round, rather than as a blanket, for the reason the
typeface section gives. The archive entries in src/site/gt/entries.json are the
project's on the only authorship record that exists for them: git, which
attributes every commit that has ever touched that file to this project. If that
is ever wrong for a particular entry, this is the file that has to say so.


WHY THIS FILE HAD TO BE WRITTEN AT ALL

The credit already existed in three places — src/site/gt/index.html (live on the
page), src/site/network.json ("Meta AI, commissioned by the card's owner"), and
the app.js comment above. None of them is a licence carve-out, and that is the
whole lesson: ATTRIBUTION IS NOT A CARVE-OUT. A person who forks this repo reads
LICENSE, receives an unrestricted MIT grant over every byte in the tree, and is
never contradicted by a credit line. Measured before this file existed: the
licence API returned spdx_id MIT for an unscoped LICENSE, and

    git ls-files | grep -iE 'licen[cs]e|notice|copying|copyright|third.?party'

matched exactly two files in 177 tracked — LICENSE and LICENSE-HARDWARE, and
LICENSE-HARDWARE grants rather than withholds. That number deserves distrust in
the direction it was used, so the pattern is printed rather than described: it
does not match OFL.txt, which IS a tracked root-level third-party licence that
scopes material out of MIT. "Nothing in this tree withholds anything" was true of
what the grep saw and false of the tree — the same blanket reflex, one level up,
in the measurement instead of the prose.

All four JPGs were served publicly, 200 with a 404 control, from both the Pages
site and raw.githubusercontent. They still are, and that is the point: the fix
for an over-claim is this file, not withdrawing the bytes the owner wants
printable. The over-claim was live and distributed, never theoretical.

The project already knew the principle and had applied it one class over. The
OFL fonts embedded in src/site/gt/index.html ship their licence WITH the bytes,
because embedding is redistribution — see the comment in tools/build_site.py that
puts OFL.txt on the site. The same reasoning reaches the artwork embedded in the
same file, and did not reach it, because the fonts arrived with terms attached
and the artwork arrived with a thank-you.

An agent that finds this file and wants to "clean it up" into LICENSE: read the
second section again. That edit has already been made once, by someone acting in
good faith, and it broke the machine-readable grant for the whole repository.
